Root cause:
The win condition treats hash() equality as if it implied value equality. It does not: hashing is many-to-one by design, and CPython's reserved value at -1 makes a two-element collision trivially reachable (CWE-697, Incorrect Comparison). The sanitizers also blacklist specific operator characters instead of allowlisting, so ~ slips through.

Defensive takeaways:
Never use hash() equality as an identity or integrity check; it is not collision-free and is not stable across processes (PYTHONHASHSEED). Compare values directly, or use a cryptographic digest for integrity.
Allowlist permitted input rather than blacklisting operators — a blacklist missed ~.
Avoid eval() on any attacker-influenced string, even truncated and filtered; parse the intended value explicitly instead.

Key lessons:
hash(-1) == -2 is a CPython implementation detail worth remembering for comparison-logic challenges.
A returncode × mode primitive turns a program's exit codes into attacker-chosen integers; grep's 0/1/2 convention is a clean source.

